#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""pi-task: hand subtasks to Qwen in pi sessions you can watch, and talk to them like a user.

Each session is one pi conversation. Its pi TUI runs in a tmux session, so it
keeps working when the calling command times out or exits, and when its window
closes. `new` opens a gnome-terminal window "pi-task: NAME" on that TUI;
--hidden skips it and `show NAME` opens one later. A pi extension
(pi-task-extension.ts) is the control channel: it takes prompts, follow-ups,
interrupts and stops from a queue, and records state, answers and token usage.
A follow-up sent while pi works reaches it after the current tool calls, the
way a steering message typed in the TUI does; --after holds it until pi has
finished. Default model: qwen3.8-flash-next when served, else pi's default,
else another served Qwen model. A served model asked for explicitly wins.
Default thinking: the
highest level the model maps.

commands:
  new [opts] PROMPT          start a session, wait, print the answer
  send NAME [opts] TEXT      send a follow-up, wait, print the answer
  wait NAME                  wait until the session is idle, print the answer
                             to the last message
  wait-many NAME [NAME ...] --timeout SEC --json [--reply-chars N] [--output FILE]
                             observe up to 32 frozen messages locally; stop waiting
                             when all settle, one fails, or the deadline expires
  result NAME                print the answer to the last message, no waiting
  status NAME | list | log NAME [-f]
                             inspect a session and read what it did
  interrupt NAME             stop the current run, like Esc; the session stays
  stop NAME [--force]        drop queued messages, end pi, close its windows
  set NAME [-m M] [-e E]     change model or thinking level, at once
  compact NAME [FOCUS]       compact the conversation
  show NAME | hide NAME      open a window on the session, or close its windows
  attach NAME                watch and type in this terminal (tmux attach)
  models [--json]            list the models the providers serve now
  models --wait [--interval SEC] [--timeout SEC]
                             like models, but probe every --interval seconds
                             (default 60) until a Qwen model is served, then
                             print and exit 0; if --timeout runs out first,
                             exit 4
  rm NAME...                 forget sessions (pi's session files stay)
  recover [--dry-run] [NAME...]
                             restart the sessions that a crash or reboot cut
  cap [N] [-m MODEL] [--json] show each model's cap and use; N sets the default
                             cap, or MODEL's own cap with -m
  with-slot --model MODEL --name LABEL -- COMMAND [ARG...]
                             run one external caller under the same cap/FIFO;
                             no provider probe, inherited stdio, command status

Text comes from the arguments, from -f FILE, or from stdin. Use stdin for long
or quoted text:
  pi-task new -C ~/repo <<'EOF'
  Find why test_foo fails. Report the root cause with file:line.
  EOF

waiting:
  new, send, wait and compact block until pi is idle: no run, no compaction,
  no queued message. The answer, pi's last message of the run, goes to stdout.
  The session name and one status line go to stderr. --bg returns at once (new
  then prints the name). --timeout SEC stops waiting after SEC seconds and
  exits 3; the session keeps working and `pi-task wait NAME` picks it up.
  Agents whose shell tool has a time limit: pass --timeout below it.

availability:
  new, send, set and recover first ask each provider in ~/.pi/agent/models.json
  for its /v1/models (PI_TASK_PROBE_TIMEOUT, default 3 s). When no Qwen model
  is served they exit 4 with one line on stderr: fall back to claude-task.
  models --wait probes on that schedule until a Qwen model is served, then
  prints what models prints and exits 0; run it in the background to be told
  when Qwen is back. --timeout SEC (default: forever) ends it with exit 4.

the cap on sessions in use:
  At most CAP sessions per model are in use on this PC, every caller's counted;
  in use means a run or a compaction is on. CAP is in
  ~/.local/state/pi-task/max-active (default 6); `cap N` sets the default for
  every model without its own cap. `cap N -m MODEL` sets a model's cap.
  The Kyiv schedule sets 6 by day (08:00–17:45), 12 at night; qwen3.8-flash-next
  gets 33 around the clock. `cap` shows both caps and each model's use.
  A message that would start a run on an idle session (new, send, recover) and
  a compaction wait in the queue for a free slot, first come first served
  within its model. list and status show such a session as waiting, with "K of
  CAP in use"; new, send, compact and wait keep waiting, and --timeout exits 3
  as before. Steers and follow-ups to a session in use go in at once;
  interrupt drops a message that waits. A run typed in pi's window counts
  too. Before a message or a compaction, pi-task restarts an idle pi whose
  extension predates the cap, on the same conversation.

exit status (session commands): 0 done, 1 error, the run ended in an error, or Qwen gave no answer,
  2 usage error, 3 still working, 4 no Qwen model is served.
  with-slot returns its command's exit status, or 128 + signal on cancellation.

notes:
  - Every session is told in its system prompt that it is a delegated session,
    which overrides the delegation advice in AGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md.
  - Sessions run with all of pi's tools and no approval prompts. A folder under
    ~/Documents/Projects (PI_TASK_TRUSTED_ROOTS) gets --approve, so pi loads its
    .pi resources; elsewhere --no-approve, so pi never stops at a trust question.
  - Slash commands are TUI commands; pi-task sends text. `send NAME /compact`
    compacts; use `set` for the model and thinking level.
  - NAME may be a unique prefix, the pi session ID, or "last".
  - PI_TASK_MODEL sets the default when qwen3.8-flash-next is unavailable;
    PI_TASK_HIDDEN=1 makes --hidden the default.
  - State: ~/.local/state/pi-task. Conversations: ~/.pi/agent/sessions.
    tmux: `tmux -L pi-task ls`.
"""

